\fR\fP.SS "Installing"
.SS "Package manager"
Using npm:

.PP
.PP
.nf
$ npm install axios
.fi
.PP

.PP
Using bower:

.PP
.PP
.nf
$ bower install axios
.fi
.PP

.PP
Using yarn:

.PP
.PP
.nf
$ yarn add axios
.fi
.PP

.PP
Using pnpm:

.PP
.PP
.nf
$ pnpm add axios
.fi
.PP

.PP
Using bun:

.PP
.PP
.nf
$ bun add axios
.fi
.PP

.PP
Once the package is installed, you can import the library using \fRimport\fP or \fRrequire\fP approach:

.PP
.PP
.nf
import axios, {isCancel, AxiosError} from 'axios';
.fi
.PP

.PP
You can also use the default export, since the named export is just a re-export from the Axios factory:

.PP
.PP
.nf
import axios from 'axios';

console\&.log(axios\&.isCancel('something'));
````

If you use `require` for importing, **only default export is available**:
.fi
.PP
 js const axios = require('axios');

.PP
console\&.log(axios\&.isCancel('something')); 
.PP
.nf
For some bundlers and some ES6 linters you may need to do the following:

.fi
.PP
 js import { default as axios } from 'axios'; 
.PP
.nf
For cases where something went wrong when trying to import a module into a custom or legacy environment,
you can try importing the module package directly:

.fi
.PP
 js const axios = require('axios/dist/browser/axios\&.cjs'); // browser commonJS bundle (ES2017) // const axios = require('axios/dist/node/axios\&.cjs'); // node commonJS bundle (ES2017) 
.PP
.nf
### CDN

Using jsDelivr CDN (ES5 UMD browser module):

.fi
.PP
 html <script src="https://cdn\&.jsdelivr\&.net/npm/axios@1\&.6\&.7/dist/axios\&.min\&.js"></script> 
.PP
.nf
Using unpkg CDN:

.fi
.PP
 html <script src="https://unpkg\&.com/axios@1\&.6\&.7/dist/axios\&.min\&.js"></script> 
.PP
.nf
## Example

> **Note**: CommonJS usage  
> In order to gain the TypeScript typings (for intellisense / autocomplete) while using CommonJS imports with `require()`, use the following approach:

.fi
.PP
 js import axios from 'axios'; //const axios = require('axios'); // legacy way

.PP
// Make a request for a user with a given ID axios\&.get('/user?ID=12345') \&.then(function (response) { // handle success console\&.log(response); }) \&.catch(function (error) { // handle error console\&.log(error); }) \&.finally(function () { // always executed });

.PP
// Optionally the request above could also be done as axios\&.get('/user', { params: { ID: 12345 } }) \&.then(function (response) { console\&.log(response); }) \&.catch(function (error) { console\&.log(error); }) \&.finally(function () { // always executed });

.PP
// Want to use async/await? Add the \fRasync\fP keyword to your outer function/method\&. async function getUser() { try { const response = await axios\&.get('/user?ID=12345'); console\&.log(response); } catch (error) { console\&.error(error); } } 
.PP
.nf
> **Note**: `async/await` is part of ECMAScript 2017 and is not supported in Internet
> Explorer and older browsers, so use with caution\&.

Performing a `POST` request

.fi
.PP
 js axios\&.post('/user', { firstName: 'Fred', lastName: 'Flintstone' }) \&.then(function (response) { console\&.log(response); }) \&.catch(function (error) { console\&.log(error); }); 
.PP
.nf
Performing multiple concurrent requests

.fi
.PP
 js function getUserAccount() { return axios\&.get('/user/12345'); }

.PP
function getUserPermissions() { return axios\&.get('/user/12345/permissions'); }

.PP
Promise\&.all([getUserAccount(), getUserPermissions()]) \&.then(function (results) { const acct = results[0]; const perm = results[1]; }); 
.PP
.nf
## axios API

Requests can be made by passing the relevant config to `axios`\&.

##### axios(config)

.fi
.PP
 js // Send a POST request axios({ method: 'post', url: '/user/12345', data: { firstName: 'Fred', lastName: 'Flintstone' } }); 
.PP
.nf

.fi
.PP
 js // GET request for remote image in node\&.js axios({ method: 'get', url: 'https://bit.ly/2mTM3nY', responseType: 'stream' }) \&.then(function (response) { response\&.data\&.pipe(fs\&.createWriteStream('ada_lovelace\&.jpg')) }); 
.PP
.nf
##### axios(url[, config])

.fi
.PP
 js // Send a GET request (default method) axios('/user/12345'); 
.PP
.nf
### Request method aliases

For convenience, aliases have been provided for all common request methods\&.

##### axios\&.request(config)
##### axios\&.get(url[, config])
##### axios\&.delete(url[, config])
##### axios\&.head(url[, config])
##### axios\&.options(url[, config])
##### axios\&.post(url[, data[, config]])
##### axios\&.put(url[, data[, config]])
##### axios\&.patch(url[, data[, config]])

###### NOTE
When using the alias methods `url`, `method`, and `data` properties don't need to be specified in config\&.

### Concurrency (Deprecated)
Please use `Promise\&.all` to replace the below functions\&.

Helper functions for dealing with concurrent requests\&.

axios\&.all(iterable)
axios\&.spread(callback)

### Creating an instance

You can create a new instance of axios with a custom config\&.

##### axios\&.create([config])

.fi
.PP
 js const instance = axios\&.create({ baseURL: 'https://some-domain.com/api/', timeout: 1000, headers: {'X-Custom-Header': 'foobar'} }); 
.PP
.nf
### Instance methods

The available instance methods are listed below\&. The specified config will be merged with the instance config\&.

##### axios#request(config)
##### axios#get(url[, config])
##### axios#delete(url[, config])
##### axios#head(url[, config])
##### axios#options(url[, config])
##### axios#post(url[, data[, config]])
##### axios#put(url[, data[, config]])
##### axios#patch(url[, data[, config]])
##### axios#getUri([config])

## Request Config

These are the available config options for making requests\&. Only the `url` is required\&. Requests will default to `GET` if `method` is not specified\&.

.fi
.PP
 js { // \fRurl\fP is the server URL that will be used for the request url: '/user',

.PP
// \fRmethod\fP is the request method to be used when making the request method: 'get', // default

.PP
// \fRbaseURL\fP will be prepended to \fRurl\fP unless \fRurl\fP is absolute and option \fRallowAbsoluteUrls\fP is set to true\&. // It can be convenient to set \fRbaseURL\fP for an instance of axios to pass relative URLs // to methods of that instance\&. baseURL: 'https://some-domain.com/api/',

.PP
// \fRallowAbsoluteUrls\fP determines whether or not absolute URLs will override a configured \fRbaseUrl\fP\&. // When set to true (default), absolute values for \fRurl\fP will override \fRbaseUrl\fP\&. // When set to false, absolute values for \fRurl\fP will always be prepended by \fRbaseUrl\fP\&. allowAbsoluteUrls: true,

.PP
// \fRtransformRequest\fP allows changes to the request data before it is sent to the server // This is only applicable for request methods 'PUT', 'POST', 'PATCH' and 'DELETE' // The last function in the array must return a string or an instance of Buffer, ArrayBuffer, // FormData or Stream // You may modify the headers object\&. transformRequest: [function (data, headers) { // Do whatever you want to transform the data

.PP
return data; }],

.PP
// \fRtransformResponse\fP allows changes to the response data to be made before // it is passed to then/catch transformResponse: [function (data) { // Do whatever you want to transform the data

.PP
return data; }],

.PP
// \fRheaders\fP are custom headers to be sent headers: {'X-Requested-With': 'XMLHttpRequest'},

.PP
// \fRparams\fP are the URL parameters to be sent with the request // Must be a plain object or a URLSearchParams object params: { ID: 12345 },

.PP
// \fRparamsSerializer\fP is an optional config that allows you to customize serializing \fRparams\fP\&. paramsSerializer: {

.PP
// Custom encoder function which sends key/value pairs in an iterative fashion\&. encode?: (param: string): string => { /* Do custom operations here and return transformed string */ },

.PP
// Custom serializer function for the entire parameter\&. Allows user to mimic pre 1\&.x behaviour\&. serialize?: (params: Record<string, any>, options?: ParamsSerializerOptions ),

.PP
// Configuration for formatting array indexes in the params\&. indexes: false // Three available options: (1) indexes: null (leads to no brackets), (2) (default) indexes: false (leads to empty brackets), (3) indexes: true (leads to brackets with indexes)\&. 
.br
 },

.PP
// \fRdata\fP is the data to be sent as the request body // Only applicable for request methods 'PUT', 'POST', 'DELETE , and 'PATCH' // When no \fRtransformRequest\fP is set, must be of one of the following types: // - string, plain object, ArrayBuffer, ArrayBufferView, URLSearchParams // - Browser only: FormData, File, Blob // - Node only: Stream, Buffer, FormData (form-data package) data: { firstName: 'Fred' },

.PP
// syntax alternative to send data into the body // method post // only the value is sent, not the key data: 'Country=Brasil&City=Belo Horizonte',

.PP
// \fRtimeout\fP specifies the number of milliseconds before the request times out\&. // If the request takes longer than \fRtimeout\fP, the request will be aborted\&. timeout: 1000, // default is \fR0\fP (no timeout)

.PP
// \fRwithCredentials\fP indicates whether or not cross-site Access-Control requests // should be made using credentials withCredentials: false, // default

.PP
// \fRadapter\fP allows custom handling of requests which makes testing easier\&. // Return a promise and supply a valid response (see lib/adapters/README\&.md) adapter: function (config) { /* \&.\&.\&. */ }, // Also, you can set the name of the built-in adapter, or provide an array with their names // to choose the first available in the environment adapter: 'xhr', // 'fetch' | 'http' | ['xhr', 'http', 'fetch']

.PP
// \fRauth\fP indicates that HTTP Basic auth should be used, and supplies credentials\&. // This will set an \fRAuthorization\fP header, overwriting any existing // \fRAuthorization\fP custom headers you have set using \fRheaders\fP\&. // Please note that only HTTP Basic auth is configurable through this parameter\&. // For Bearer tokens and such, use \fRAuthorization\fP custom headers instead\&. auth: { username: 'janedoe', password: 's00pers3cret' },

.PP
// \fRresponseType\fP indicates the type of data that the server will respond with // options are: 'arraybuffer', 'document', 'json', 'text', 'stream' // browser only: 'blob' responseType: 'json', // default

.PP
// \fRresponseEncoding\fP indicates encoding to use for decoding responses (Node\&.js only) // Note: Ignored for \fRresponseType\fP of 'stream' or client-side requests // options are: 'ascii', 'ASCII', 'ansi', 'ANSI', 'binary', 'BINARY', 'base64', 'BASE64', 'base64url', // 'BASE64URL', 'hex', 'HEX', 'latin1', 'LATIN1', 'ucs-2', 'UCS-2', 'ucs2', 'UCS2', 'utf-8', 'UTF-8', // 'utf8', 'UTF8', 'utf16le', 'UTF16LE' responseEncoding: 'utf8', // default

.PP
// \fRxsrfCookieName\fP is the name of the cookie to use as a value for xsrf token xsrfCookieName: 'XSRF-TOKEN', // default

.PP
// \fRxsrfHeaderName\fP is the name of the http header that carries the xsrf token value xsrfHeaderName: 'X-XSRF-TOKEN', // default

.PP
// \fRundefined\fP (default) - set XSRF header only for the same origin requests withXSRFToken: boolean | undefined | ((config: InternalAxiosRequestConfig) => boolean | undefined),

.PP
// \fRonUploadProgress\fP allows handling of progress events for uploads // browser & node\&.js onUploadProgress: function ({loaded, total, progress, bytes, estimated, rate, upload = true}) { // Do whatever you want with the Axios progress event },

.PP
// \fRonDownloadProgress\fP allows handling of progress events for downloads // browser & node\&.js onDownloadProgress: function ({loaded, total, progress, bytes, estimated, rate, download = true}) { // Do whatever you want with the Axios progress event },

.PP
// \fRmaxContentLength\fP defines the max size of the http response content in bytes allowed in node\&.js maxContentLength: 2000,

.PP
// \fRmaxBodyLength\fP (Node only option) defines the max size of the http request content in bytes allowed maxBodyLength: 2000,

.PP
// \fRvalidateStatus\fP defines whether to resolve or reject the promise for a given // HTTP response status code\&. If \fRvalidateStatus\fP returns \fRtrue\fP (or is set to \fRnull\fP // or \fRundefined\fP), the promise will be resolved; otherwise, the promise will be // rejected\&. validateStatus: function (status) { return status >= 200 && status < 300; // default },

.PP
// \fRmaxRedirects\fP defines the maximum number of redirects to follow in node\&.js\&. // If set to 0, no redirects will be followed\&. maxRedirects: 21, // default

.PP
// \fRbeforeRedirect\fP defines a function that will be called before redirect\&. // Use this to adjust the request options upon redirecting, // to inspect the latest response headers, // or to cancel the request by throwing an error // If maxRedirects is set to 0, \fRbeforeRedirect\fP is not used\&. beforeRedirect: (options, { headers }) => { if (options\&.hostname === "example\&.com") { options\&.auth = "user:password"; } },

.PP
// \fRsocketPath\fP defines a UNIX Socket to be used in node\&.js\&. // e\&.g\&. '/var/run/docker\&.sock' to send requests to the docker daemon\&. // Only either \fRsocketPath\fP or \fRproxy\fP can be specified\&. // If both are specified, \fRsocketPath\fP is used\&. socketPath: null, // default

.PP
// \fRtransport\fP determines the transport method that will be used to make the request\&. // If defined, it will be used\&. Otherwise, if \fRmaxRedirects\fP is 0, // the default \fRhttp\fP or \fRhttps\fP library will be used, depending on the protocol specified in \fRprotocol\fP\&. // Otherwise, the \fRhttpFollow\fP or \fRhttpsFollow\fP library will be used, again depending on the protocol, // which can handle redirects\&. transport: undefined, // default

.PP
// \fRhttpAgent\fP and \fRhttpsAgent\fP define a custom agent to be used when performing http // and https requests, respectively, in node\&.js\&. This allows options to be added like // \fRkeepAlive\fP that are not enabled by default before Node\&.js v19\&.0\&.0\&. After Node\&.js // v19\&.0\&.0, you no longer need to customize the agent to enable \fRkeepAlive\fP because // \fRhttp\&.globalAgent\fP has \fRkeepAlive\fP enabled by default\&. httpAgent: new http\&.Agent({ keepAlive: true }), httpsAgent: new https\&.Agent({ keepAlive: true }),

.PP
// \fRproxy\fP defines the hostname, port, and protocol of the proxy server\&. // You can also define your proxy using the conventional \fRhttp_proxy\fP and // \fRhttps_proxy\fP environment variables\&. If you are using environment variables // for your proxy configuration, you can also define a \fRno_proxy\fP environment // variable as a comma-separated list of domains that should not be proxied\&. // Use \fRfalse\fP to disable proxies, ignoring environment variables\&. // \fRauth\fP indicates that HTTP Basic auth should be used to connect to the proxy, and // supplies credentials\&. // This will set an \fRProxy-Authorization\fP header, overwriting any existing // \fRProxy-Authorization\fP custom headers you have set using \fRheaders\fP\&. // If the proxy server uses HTTPS, then you must set the protocol to \fRhttps\fP\&. proxy: { protocol: 'https', host: '127\&.0\&.0\&.1', // hostname: '127\&.0\&.0\&.1' // Takes precedence over 'host' if both are defined port: 9000, auth: { username: 'mikeymike', password: 'rapunz3l' } },

.PP
// \fRcancelToken\fP specifies a cancel token that can be used to cancel the request // (see Cancellation section below for details) cancelToken: new CancelToken(function (cancel) { }),

.PP
// an alternative way to cancel Axios requests using AbortController signal: new AbortController()\&.signal,

.PP
// \fRdecompress\fP indicates whether or not the response body should be decompressed // automatically\&. If set to \fRtrue\fP will also remove the 'content-encoding' header // from the responses objects of all decompressed responses // - Node only (XHR cannot turn off decompression) decompress: true, // default

.PP
// \fRinsecureHTTPParser\fP boolean\&. // Indicates where to use an insecure HTTP parser that accepts invalid HTTP headers\&. // This may allow interoperability with non-conformant HTTP implementations\&. // Using the insecure parser should be avoided\&. // see options https://nodejs.org/dist/latest-v12.x/docs/api/http.html#http_http_request_url_options_callback // see also https://nodejs.org/en/blog/vulnerability/february-2020-security-releases/#strict-http-header-parsing-none insecureHTTPParser: undefined, // default

.PP
// transitional options for backward compatibility that may be removed in the newer versions transitional: { // silent JSON parsing mode // \fRtrue\fP - ignore JSON parsing errors and set response\&.data to null if parsing failed (old behaviour) // \fRfalse\fP - throw SyntaxError if JSON parsing failed (Note: responseType must be set to 'json') silentJSONParsing: true, // default value for the current Axios version

.PP
// try to parse the response string as JSON even if \fRresponseType\fP is not 'json' forcedJSONParsing: true,

