.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRsetContentLength\fP, \fRgetContentLength\fP, \fRhasContentLength\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRsetAccept\fP, \fRgetAccept\fP, \fRhasAccept\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRsetUserAgent\fP, \fRgetUserAgent\fP, \fRhasUserAgent\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRsetContentEncoding\fP, \fRgetContentEncoding\fP, \fRhasContentEncoding\fP
.PP
.SS "🔥 Fetch adapter"
Fetch adapter was introduced in \fRv1\&.7\&.0\fP\&. By default, it will be used if \fRxhr\fP and \fRhttp\fP adapters are not available in the build, or not supported by the environment\&. To use it by default, it must be selected explicitly:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const {data} = axios\&.get(url, {
  adapter: 'fetch' // by default ['xhr', 'http', 'fetch']
})
.fi
.PP

.PP
You can create a separate instance for this:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const fetchAxios = axios\&.create({
  adapter: 'fetch'
});

const {data} = fetchAxios\&.get(url);
.fi
.PP

.PP
The adapter supports the same functionality as \fRxhr\fP adapter, \fBincluding upload and download progress capturing\fP\&. Also, it supports additional response types such as \fRstream\fP and \fRformdata\fP (if supported by the environment)\&.
.SS "🔥 Custom fetch"
Starting from \fRv1\&.12\&.0\fP, you can customize the fetch adapter to use a custom fetch API instead of environment globals\&. You can pass a custom \fRfetch\fP function, \fRRequest\fP, and \fRResponse\fP constructors via env config\&. This can be helpful in case of custom environments & app frameworks\&.

.PP
Also, when using a custom fetch, you may need to set custom Request and Response too\&. If you don't set them, global objects will be used\&. If your custom fetch api does not have these objects, and the globals are incompatible with a custom fetch, you must disable their use inside the fetch adapter by passing null\&.

.PP
.RS 4
Note: Setting \fRRequest\fP & \fRResponse\fP to \fRnull\fP will make it impossible for the fetch adapter to capture the upload & download progress\&. 
.RE
.PP

.PP
Basic example:

.PP
.PP
.nf
import customFetchFunction from 'customFetchModule';

const instance = axios\&.create({
  adapter: 'fetch',
  onDownloadProgress(e) {
    console\&.log('downloadProgress', e);
  },
  env: {
    fetch: customFetchFunction,
    Request: null, // undefined \-> use the global constructor
    Response: null
  }
});
.fi
.PP
.SS "🔥 Using with Tauri"
A minimal example of setting up Axios for use in a \fRTauri\fP app with a platform fetch function that ignores CORS policy for requests\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
import { fetch } from "@tauri\-apps/plugin\-http";
import axios from "axios";

const instance = axios\&.create({
  adapter: 'fetch',
  onDownloadProgress(e) {
    console\&.log('downloadProgress', e);
  },
  env: {
    fetch
  }
});

 const {data} = await instance\&.get("https://google\&.com");
.fi
.PP
.SS "🔥 Using with SvelteKit"
\fRSvelteKit\fP framework has a custom implementation of the fetch function for server rendering (so called \fRload\fP functions), and also uses relative paths, which makes it incompatible with the standard URL API\&. So, Axios must be configured to use the custom fetch API:

.PP
.PP
.nf
export async function load({ fetch }) {
  const {data: post} = await axios\&.get('https://jsonplaceholder\&.typicode\&.com/posts/1', {
    adapter: 'fetch',
    env: {
      fetch,
      Request: null,
      Response: null
    }
  });

  return { post };
}
.fi
.PP
.SS "Semver"
Since Axios has reached a \fRv\&.1\&.0\&.0\fP we will fully embrace semver as per the spec \fRhere\fP
.SS "Promises"
axios depends on a native ES6 Promise implementation to be \fRsupported\fP\&. If your environment doesn't support ES6 Promises, you can \fRpolyfill\fP\&.
.SS "TypeScript"
axios includes \fRTypeScript\fP definitions and a type guard for axios errors\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
let user: User = null;
try {
  const { data } = await axios\&.get('/user?ID=12345');
  user = data\&.userDetails;
} catch (error) {
  if (axios\&.isAxiosError(error)) {
    handleAxiosError(error);
  } else {
    handleUnexpectedError(error);
  }
}
.fi
.PP

.PP
Because axios dual publishes with an ESM default export and a CJS \fRmodule\&.exports\fP, there are some caveats\&. The recommended setting is to use \fR"moduleResolution": "node16"\fP (this is implied by \fR"module": "node16"\fP)\&. Note that this requires TypeScript 4\&.7 or greater\&. If use ESM, your settings should be fine\&. If you compile TypeScript to CJS and you can’t use \fR"moduleResolution": "node 16"\fP, you have to enable \fResModuleInterop\fP\&. If you use TypeScript to type check CJS JavaScript code, your only option is to use \fR"moduleResolution": "node16"\fP\&.
.SS "Online one-click setup"
You can use Gitpod, an online IDE(which is free for Open Source) for contributing or running the examples online\&.

